1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,496
Under Kansas is water.

2
00:00:01,496 --> 00:00:04,488
It is older than the farms above it.

3
00:00:04,488 --> 00:00:08,228
Some of it fell as rain thirteen thousand years ago.

4
00:00:08,228 --> 00:00:11,594
It has sat in sand and gravel ever since.

5
00:00:11,594 --> 00:00:13,464
That water now feeds America.

6
00:00:13,464 --> 00:00:17,578
The aquifer under Kansas covers thirty thousand eight hundred square miles.

7
00:00:17,578 --> 00:00:23,188
It formed from sand and gravel washed off the Rocky Mountains over several million years.

8
00:00:23,188 --> 00:00:27,676
Recharge is generally less than three tenths of an inch per year.

9
00:00:27,676 --> 00:00:32,912
In southern Finney County, it is about one tenth of an inch per year.

10
00:00:32,912 --> 00:00:39,270
The High Plains aquifer lost about three hundred thirty cubic kilometres between predevelopment and two thousand seven.

11
00:00:39,270 --> 00:00:43,758
One third of that loss came from four percent of its area.

12
00:00:43,758 --> 00:00:46,376
That area is in Kansas and Texas.

13
00:00:46,376 --> 00:00:52,360
In some areas of western Kansas, levels are down by more than sixty percent since predevelopment.

14
00:00:52,360 --> 00:00:55,726
In Wallace County, the fall is about eighty percent.

15
00:00:55,726 --> 00:01:01,336
Saturated thickness of three hundred feet or more was common in southwest Kansas before irrigation.

16
00:01:01,336 --> 00:01:05,450
In many west central areas, less than thirty feet is left.

17
00:01:05,450 --> 00:01:06,946
It goes to corn.

18
00:01:06,946 --> 00:01:10,312
Centre pivots arrived in Kansas in the nineteen sixties.

19
00:01:10,312 --> 00:01:14,800
By two thousand, they watered more than eighty percent of irrigated land.

20
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:18,166
Corn is about fifty percent of Kansas irrigated acreage.

21
00:01:18,166 --> 00:01:22,280
In two thousand twenty three, Kansas grew

22
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:26,394
one million one hundred forty five thousand four hundred thirty one acres of irrigated corn.

23
00:01:26,394 --> 00:01:31,256
Each acre received an average of one point two acre feet of water.

24
00:01:31,256 --> 00:01:36,866
About forty seven percent of Kansas corn is used in state for feed and processing.

25
00:01:36,866 --> 00:01:39,110
Fifty three percent leaves the state.

26
00:01:39,110 --> 00:01:42,476
The feed and the water attracted the feedlot industry.

27
00:01:42,476 --> 00:01:43,972
The packing plants followed.

28
00:01:43,972 --> 00:01:48,086
Their need for water was met by using the Ogallala Aquifer.

29
00:01:48,086 --> 00:01:55,192
Kansas now holds two point three nine million cattle on feed in lots of one thousand head or more.

30
00:01:55,192 --> 00:01:57,810
That is third in the United States.

31
00:01:57,810 --> 00:02:01,550
It is about twenty one percent of the national total.

32
00:02:01,550 --> 00:02:06,038
A feedlot steer stays on feed for about one hundred eighty days.

33
00:02:06,038 --> 00:02:10,900
Each animal drinks about twenty five to thirty gallons of water per day.

34
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:16,510
Four beef plants in southwest Kansas can slaughter about twenty four thousand head per day.

35
00:02:16,510 --> 00:02:22,120
That is more than twenty percent of daily fed cattle slaughter in the United States.

36
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:26,982
One plant at Holcomb slaughters about five thousand six hundred cattle per day.

37
00:02:26,982 --> 00:02:32,218
A packing plant needs four hundred to six hundred gallons of water per head.

38
00:02:32,218 --> 00:02:33,714
Here is the mechanism.

39
00:02:33,714 --> 00:02:39,698
Where water levels have fallen by thirty metres or more, depletion exceeds recharge by ten times.

40
00:02:39,698 --> 00:02:41,568
The aquifer is being mined.

41
00:02:41,568 --> 00:02:45,308
It is not being used at the rate it refills.

42
00:02:45,308 --> 00:02:53,162
Cutting pumping by eighteen to thirty two percent could hold levels steady for one to two decades in the worst areas.

43
00:02:53,162 --> 00:02:55,032
Old water waters the corn.

44
00:02:55,032 --> 00:02:56,902
The corn feeds the cattle.

45
00:02:56,902 --> 00:02:58,772
The cattle feed the plants.

46
00:02:58,772 --> 00:03:00,268
The plants feed America.

47
00:03:00,268 --> 00:03:05,130
What should Kansas do with water that took thirteen thousand years to store?

48
00:03:05,130 --> 00:03:07,000
Tell us in the comments.
